     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order, even with your confirmation. The problem isn't whether the dose is what you meant. A 1000 mg daily dose of sertraline could seriously harm the patient, and a confirmation from a non-prescriber doesn't make it safe.\n\nHere's what can move this forward:\n\n- **If the dose was a mistake:** Give me the intended dose and I'll enter it. Typical adult doses are 50\u2013200 mg by mouth once daily.\n- **If a prescriber intends an unusually high dose:** The treating physician should enter the order themselves. They should first consult pharmacy and document the clinical rationale. Pharmacy and the prescriber can then check it through the hospital's high-alert medication process.\n- **If you have concerns about the patient's care:** I'd suggest talking with the attending physician or the pharmacy lead on the medicine service.\n\nI can enter a standard-dose sertraline order if you give me the dose."
      }
     ],
